-- allium: 3
-- spacetime-seed.allium
--
-- Scope: Moving the whole shared domain out of one store and into another, as
--        a single self-contained artefact. What a snapshot must contain to be
--        worth having, what restoring one is obliged to preserve, and the one
--        obligation that is invisible until it bites — that a store which has
--        had ids written into it does not know what ids it has handed out.
-- Includes: taking a snapshot of a store; restoring one; the id-sequence
--           burn that must follow a restore; what makes a second restore of
--           the same snapshot a no-op; importing an old store; the store's
--           own refusal of a task or epic row with an unknown status.
-- Retired: seeding a store from a board's local SQLite database, and dumping
--          that database to a snapshot (`dispatch spacetime seed` and
--          `dispatch spacetime dump`), removed with task #16755 — see
--          "Seeding (removed)" below.
-- Excludes: the shared schema itself and the shape of every row in it (see
--           core.allium, which owns Task, Epic, Host, SavedRepoPath and the
--           rest); which store is authoritative and why (see
--           docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-17-spacetimedb-migration-design.md);
--           the subscription model, the user identity and the connect/reconnect
--           loop (see sync.allium and host.allium); and transport — nothing here cares
--           whether a snapshot travels as JSON, as a file, or over a socket.
--
-- Why this exists as its own file, and exists FIRST. This is the escape hatch,
-- and an escape hatch written after the thing it rescues has already failed is
-- not an escape hatch. It has three jobs and they are the same job:
--
--   The backup. The shared store is a single point of failure with no
--   replication. A snapshot is the only copy.
--
--   The recovery path. The target store's migrations are append-only: removing
--   a column, reordering one, or adding a unique constraint is refused
--   outright. The way out is to dump, rebuild, and restore. Nothing else
--   offers one.
--
--   The move. Rows cross from one store to another — a restore onto a rebuilt
--   store, or an import from an old one — with their task and epic ids carried
--   over unchanged, so that every id already written into a worktree name, a
--   branch name, a plan path or a person's memory still means what it meant.
--   (This job was once "the seed": the shared store starting from one board's
--   local database. Every board has been on the store since task #4916, and
--   the seed was removed with task #16755.)
--
-- One artefact serves all three because the three want the same guarantees:
-- everything, unaltered, and restorable onto a store that then behaves as if
-- the rows had always been there. That last clause is the whole of the
-- sequence-burn obligation below, and it is the only part of this file that
-- is not obvious.

use "./core.allium" as core

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The shared domain, named table by table.
--
-- This enumeration is the definition of "complete" that SnapshotIsComplete
-- below tests against, and it is deliberately a closed list rather than
-- "whatever the store happens to hold". A snapshot that silently omitted a
-- table would restore into a store that looked healthy and had lost something,
-- and nothing about the restored board would say which. Adding a shared table
-- without adding it here is the failure this list exists to make impossible:
-- the value is absent, the completeness invariant fails, and the omission
-- surfaces at the moment the table is introduced rather than at the moment
-- somebody needs the backup.
--
-- What is NOT here is as load-bearing as what is, and it is shorter than an
-- earlier revision of this note claimed: the knowledge base (`learnings`,
-- `learning_retrievals`) and host-scoped `settings` are shared and ARE below —
-- see `docs/conventions.md`'s "The store seam" for when each joined. What
-- stays off this list, permanently, is the identity credential
-- (`IdentityCredentialStore`'s `user_identity_token`): it is the proof this
-- install presents to authenticate, not a row the store could hold without
-- circularity, so it lives in the host file instead (host.allium:
-- IdentityLivesInHostFile). Not an oversight; that split is the table
-- partition in the migration design.
--
-- Three members are dead: `task_shells` (shell tracking, removed in #4965),
-- `todos` (the TODO overlay, removed in #4970), and `filter_presets` (saved
-- repo-filter presets, removed in #4972). The store automigrates a removed
-- table only while it is empty: a table holding any row refuses the publish
-- ("table contains data"), and even an empty one disconnects every client
-- (checked on SpacetimeDB 2.10.1 in task #4972). So all three stay in the
-- module, never written, and a dump of any of them is always empty. They stay
-- in this list because the store still holds them and
-- "complete" means every table it holds.
enum SharedTable {
    tasks
    epics
    todos
    task_watchers
    task_shells
    task_subagents
    repo_paths
    repo_base_branches
    hosts
    subscriptions
    poll_owners
    settings
    filter_presets
    learnings
    learning_retrievals
    usage_events
    retired_feed_items
}

-- Why a restore refused a snapshot. Every arm is a refusal to write anything
-- at all, which is the point: a partially-restored store is worse than an
-- untouched one, because it looks like a board.
--
-- A file that cannot be read as a snapshot at all is deliberately not an arm
-- here. It never becomes a Snapshot, so no rule below can bind it, and giving
-- it a reason code would describe a refusal this spec does not govern. It is
-- an ordinary unreadable-input failure of whatever loads the file.
--
-- store_not_empty was the seed's own refusal (a seed into a store that
-- already held tasks or epics) and went with the seed (task #16755).
enum RefusalReason {
    format_unsupported    -- written by a version of the tool this one cannot read
    schema_mismatch       -- the rows describe a schema this store does not have
    incomplete            -- the snapshot does not cover every shared table, or an id ceiling is implausible (RefuseImplausibleCeiling)
    archived_rows_present -- a task or epic row still carries the retired `archived` status
    id_conflict           -- an import found a task, epic or learning whose id is already taken by a DIFFERENT row (ImportOldStore)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What an operator is told when a restore refuses.
--
-- The reason is a closed set so that a caller can branch on it; the detail is
-- free text so that a human can act on it. Both are carried, because the
-- RefusalIsSilentAboutNothing guarantee is unmeetable with only one: a reason
-- code alone does not say WHICH table was missing or WHICH version was found,
-- and free text alone cannot be tested against.
value Refusal {
    reason: RefusalReason
    detail: String
}

-- One table's contribution to a snapshot.
--
-- `rows` is deliberately opaque. This spec does not describe the shape of a
-- task row — core.allium does — and a second description here would be a
-- second thing to keep in step, which is exactly the duplication the whole
-- migration exists to remove. What this spec constrains is that the rows are
-- carried WHOLE and UNALTERED, which is a property of the extract, not of any
-- field in it.
--
-- `row_ids` is separated out because the ids are the one part of a row this
-- spec reasons about directly: they are what must survive the round trip, and
-- they are what the sequence burn is measured against.
--
-- `columns` is what makes the rows interpretable on the far side. It sits on
-- the extract rather than as one schema block on the Snapshot because each
-- extract already names its table, and the columns are a property of that
-- table's rows — putting them anywhere else would mean carrying the table name
-- twice and keeping the two lists in step by hand. It is also what a restore
-- actually compares against: the question at restore time is asked per table
-- ("can these rows go into this table?"), never about the schema as a whole.
--
-- NOT EVERY SHARED TABLE HAS ONE. Half the shared domain is identified by
-- something the store never generates: a task's live shells are keyed by the
-- task and the shell's own id, a saved repo by its path, a branch by the pair
-- it names. Those rows have nothing to preserve and nothing to burn, so their
-- extract carries no ids at all, and they have no IdSequence. This is a real
-- distinction rather than a gap in the data: an extract with no ids says "this
-- table does not generate them", which is why the invariant below admits the
-- empty case rather than demanding a placeholder per row.
value TableExtract {
    table: SharedTable
    rows: List<String>          -- each row encoded whole; opaque to this spec
    row_ids: List<Integer>      -- the generated id of each row, in the same order; empty for a table whose identity is not generated
    columns: List<String>       -- the names of the columns those rows carry

    row_count: rows.count

    -- An extract that lost track of which row is which is not an extract. This
    -- is the structural half of id preservation: the ids travel alongside the
    -- rows rather than being re-derived on the far side, so a restore never has
    -- to guess. Either every row is accounted for or the table has no generated
    -- ids to account for; a partial list is the one state that means nothing.
    invariant EveryRowCarriesItsId {
        row_ids.count = 0 or row_ids.count = rows.count
    }

    -- An extract that names no columns makes no claim about its schema, and
    -- "no claim" must never be allowed to read as "matches". A set-comparison
    -- against an empty list is the shape that quietly passes some checks and
    -- fails others, so the empty case is ruled out here rather than guarded at
    -- every site that compares. A table with no rows still has columns; see
    -- TakeSnapshot's guidance for why they are recorded rather than derived.
    invariant EveryExtractNamesItsColumns {
        columns.count > 0
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A snapshot of the entire shared domain at one moment.
--
-- "At one moment" is the guarantee that makes a snapshot restorable at all.
-- The board is live while a dump runs: agents are writing, feeds are upserting,
-- hooks are flipping counters. A snapshot assembled from ten separately-timed
-- reads could hold a watch whose task is absent, or a task pointing at an epic
-- that was created after the epics table was read — and the restore would
-- either fail on the reference or, worse, succeed and leave an orphan. See
-- TakeSnapshot below for what this obliges.
entity Snapshot {
    format_version: Integer     -- the snapshot format itself, bumped when the file layout changes
    taken_at: Timestamp
    extracts: List<TableExtract>

    highest_id(t): extracts.filter(e => e.table = t).sum(e => e.row_ids.max ?? 0)

    -- Completeness is table-level, not row-level: every shared table appears,
    -- even when it appears empty. An empty extract and a missing extract are
    -- different claims — the first says "this table had no rows", the second
    -- says nothing at all — and only the first is safe to restore from.
    invariant SnapshotIsComplete {
        extracts.count = config.shared_table_count
    }

    invariant NoTableAppearsTwice {
        for a in extracts:
            for b in extracts:
                a != b implies a.table != b.table
    }
}

-- What a store will hand out next, for one table that generates its own ids.
--
-- THIS ENTITY IS THE REASON THIS SPEC EXISTS. The shared store generates an id
-- only when the caller supplies none, and a caller that supplies one is not
-- asking for a generated id, so the counter does not move. That is a perfectly
-- reasonable rule and it is silently catastrophic for a restore: restoring ten
-- thousand tasks with their original ids leaves the counter exactly where it
-- started, and the very next task created gets id 1 — an id that a ten-year-old
-- task already holds.
--
-- There is no operation that sets a counter. The only way to move it is to
-- make the store generate values and throw them away, which is what
-- BurnIdSequence below does. The invariant is what any correct implementation
-- has to re-establish.
entity IdSequence {
    table: SharedTable
    next_value: Integer         -- the id the next generated insert will receive
    highest_row_id: Integer     -- the largest id present in that table; 0 when the table is empty

    -- The load-bearing claim of this whole file. A store that satisfies it
    -- cannot hand out an id that collides with a row it already holds,
    -- whatever route those rows arrived by.
    --
    -- Stated as a property of the store rather than as a step in the restore
    -- procedure on purpose: it must hold after a restore, after an import,
    -- after a restore-on-top-of-a-restore, and in the ordinary case where no restore
    -- has ever happened and it holds for free. An implementation that satisfies
    -- it by burning the counter and one that satisfies it some other way are
    -- both correct; one that reports success without satisfying it is not.
    invariant SequenceClearsEveryRow {
        next_value > highest_row_id
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- How many tables SnapshotIsComplete expects. Kept as a parameter rather
    -- than written into the invariant so that the number and the SharedTable
    -- enumeration are visibly two statements of the same fact, and disagreeing
    -- is a spec-level error rather than a silent gap in a backup.
    shared_table_count: Integer = 17

    -- Bumped when the snapshot layout changes in a way an older reader cannot
    -- interpret. A reader refuses a version it does not know rather than
    -- guessing — see RefuseUnsupportedSnapshot.
    --
    -- Version 2: every extract carries the names of its columns, and the
    -- integer schema version the artefact used to record is gone. A format-1
    -- snapshot is therefore refused as format_unsupported, and that is
    -- deliberate rather than collateral: it never recorded its columns, so
    -- there is nothing to check it against under the new rule, and the only
    -- alternatives are guessing or restoring unchecked.
    snapshot_format_version: Integer = 2

    -- The largest id a burn will chase, per row that claims it, beyond which
    -- the snapshot is treated as corrupt rather than large. See
    -- RefuseImplausibleCeiling. Mirrors MAX_CEILING_PER_ROW in
    -- src/spacetime/restore.rs.
    max_ceiling_per_row: Integer = 1000000
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- == Taking a snapshot ==

rule TakeSnapshot {
    when: TakeSnapshot(operator)

    ensures:
        Snapshot.created(
            format_version: config.snapshot_format_version,
            taken_at: now,
            extracts: extract_every_shared_table()
        )

    @guidance
        -- READS ONE MOMENT, NOT TEN. The extracts must come from a single
        -- consistent read of the store — one transaction, one subscription
        -- state, one view. Not ten sequential queries. The board is live during
        -- a dump, and the failure this prevents is not a lost row: it is a
        -- snapshot that holds a watch whose task is missing, or a task whose
        -- epic is, because the two tables were read either side of a write.
        -- Such a snapshot restores cleanly and produces a board with dangling
        -- references, which is the worst outcome available here — it looks
        -- fine.
        --
        -- The cost of this is that a dump of a large board holds a read view
        -- open for its duration. That is accepted: a dump is an operator
        -- action taken deliberately, not a background job, and the alternative
        -- is a backup that cannot be trusted.
        --
        -- EVERY TABLE, INCLUDING THE EMPTY ONES. extract_every_shared_table()
        -- covers all of SharedTable; a table with no rows yields an extract
        -- with no rows, not an absent extract. SnapshotIsComplete refuses the
        -- alternative. This is what makes a restore able to distinguish "this
        -- table was empty" from "this snapshot predates this table", and the
        -- second is a refusal, not a silent empty restore.
        --
        -- ROWS TRAVEL WHOLE. No field is dropped, defaulted, recomputed or
        -- normalised on the way out. That includes fields this spec does not
        -- name and fields that look derived: a denormalised counter that is
        -- recomputed on restore rather than carried is a field whose restored
        -- value depends on the restore code being right, and the point of a
        -- backup is to depend on as little as possible.
        --
        -- THE ARTEFACT RECORDS ITS OWN COLUMNS, PER TABLE, INCLUDING FOR A
        -- TABLE WITH NO ROWS. A snapshot that did not would be a set of rows
        -- whose meaning has to be inferred, and inference is exactly what
        -- fails quietly when a column has been added or renamed since. The
        -- empty table is the case that decides the design: it has no rows to
        -- infer columns from, so a reader that derived the columns from the
        -- rows would have nothing to say about it and would have to treat
        -- "unknown" as "fine". The columns are therefore read from the table
        -- itself and written down, for every table, which is what gives
        -- RefuseMismatchedSchema something to check.
}

-- == Restoring ==

rule RestoreSnapshot {
    when: RestoreSnapshot(operator, snapshot)

    requires: snapshot.format_version = config.snapshot_format_version
    requires: not snapshot.extracts.any(e => columns_differ_as_sets(e.columns, store_columns(e.table)))
    requires: snapshot.extracts.count = config.shared_table_count
    requires: not snapshot.extracts.any(e => (e.table = tasks or e.table = epics) and e.rows.any(r => row_status(r) = "archived"))
    requires: not snapshot.extracts.any(e => snapshot.highest_id(e.table) < 0 or snapshot.highest_id(e.table) > e.row_count * config.max_ceiling_per_row)

    ensures:
        BurnIdSequences(snapshot: snapshot)
        for extract in snapshot.extracts:
            restore_rows_preserving_ids(extract)

    @guidance
        -- IDS ARE DATA, NOT ADDRESSES. Every row is written with the id it had.
        -- Not a fresh id, not a remapped one, not a dense renumbering that
        -- closes the gaps left by deleted tasks. Task ids are quoted in
        -- worktree directory names, branch names, plan paths, commit messages,
        -- PR titles and the heads of the people who work here. Renumbering
        -- would break all of that in a way no test would catch, because every
        -- internal reference would still be self-consistent.
        --
        -- Gaps are preserved for the same reason: a gap is evidence that a task
        -- existed and does not, and closing it makes deleted work look like it
        -- never happened.
        --
        -- WRITES MATCH BY ID, SO A SECOND RESTORE IS A NO-OP. Restoring is not
        -- appending. A row whose id is already present is overwritten with the
        -- snapshot's version of it; a row whose id is not present is inserted.
        -- Two consequences, both wanted: restoring the same snapshot twice
        -- leaves the store exactly as the first restore left it, and a restore
        -- interrupted halfway can simply be run again. An append-shaped restore
        -- would give a duplicate-key failure at best and a duplicated board at
        -- worst, and would make recovery-from-a-failed-recovery a new problem.
        --
        -- Note what idempotency does NOT claim: rows present in the store but
        -- absent from the snapshot are not removed. A restore reinstates what
        -- the snapshot holds; it does not make the store equal to the snapshot.
        -- Making that distinction disappear — a restore that also deletes — is
        -- a different and far more dangerous operation, and it is not
        -- specified here. See the open question below.
        --
        -- RESUMABLE, NOT ATOMIC, and the difference is deliberate rather than a
        -- shortfall. All-or-nothing is unachievable here and saying otherwise
        -- would be worse than not claiming it: a snapshot's rows do not fit in
        -- one call to the store, so a restore is many transactions however it
        -- is written, and no amount of care at this level changes that.
        --
        -- What makes a half-finished restore safe is a different property, and
        -- one this rule really does have: every write matches on the key, and
        -- the burn is a no-op once the counter is clear. So a restore that died
        -- partway is finished by running it again — it does not have to be
        -- undone first, and running it twice is indistinguishable from running
        -- it once. The refusal rules below are what keep the store untouched in
        -- the cases that CAN be detected before writing; resumability is what
        -- covers the rest.
        --
        -- THE BURN IS NOT OPTIONAL AND NOT SEPARATE. It is chained here rather
        -- than left to the operator as a second command, because a restore
        -- whose burn was forgotten produces a store that works perfectly until
        -- somebody creates a task, and then produces a collision whose cause is
        -- hours of git archaeology away from its symptom.
        --
        -- THE BURN COMES FIRST, AND THE ORDER IS NOT A PREFERENCE. Burning
        -- means asking the store to generate ids 1, 2, 3 and so on, which are
        -- exactly the ids this rule is about to write. Against a table that
        -- already holds them, every one of those inserts lands on an existing
        -- primary key; the store rejects it, and a reducer whose insert is
        -- rejected aborts. There is no way to advance a counter past a row
        -- without generating that row's id, so burning a loaded table is not
        -- slow, it is impossible. Against an empty table it is neither.
        --
        -- This contradicts the migration design, which says to burn after
        -- loading. The design is wrong on this point and this file is the
        -- correction; it was found by writing the restore, not by reading it.
}

rule BurnIdSequences {
    when: BurnIdSequences(snapshot)

    for sequence in IdSequences:
        let restored_ceiling = snapshot.highest_id(sequence.table)

        ensures:
            sequence.next_value > restored_ceiling

    @guidance
        -- WHAT THIS COSTS. There is no operation that sets a counter, so the
        -- only way to advance one is to make the store generate ids and discard
        -- them: insert a row asking for a generated id, delete it, repeat until
        -- the generated value passes the highest restored id. It is a loop that
        -- exists solely to consume numbers, and it costs one insert and one
        -- delete PER ID, not per row — a board whose highest task id is twenty
        -- thousand burns twenty thousand of them.
        --
        -- Stated plainly because it is easy to assume otherwise: the store
        -- pre-allocates ids in blocks, but a block is reserved storage, not a
        -- shortcut past generating each value, so it does not shorten this
        -- loop. What makes the cost acceptable is that it runs once, inside one
        -- reducer, against an empty table, at recovery or import time — never on
        -- a path anybody waits on twice.
        --
        -- Gaps in the id space are expected and harmless: nothing in the domain
        -- reads an id as a count or a position.
        --
        -- STRICTLY GREATER, NOT EQUAL. The counter must pass the highest
        -- restored id, not reach it: a counter sitting exactly on the highest
        -- id hands that id out next. The off-by-one here is the entire bug this
        -- rule exists to prevent, and it is the one that survives a casual
        -- reading of the code.
        --
        -- MEASURED AGAINST THE SNAPSHOT, PER TABLE. Each table has its own
        -- counter and its own highest id; burning one does not help another.
        -- A table whose extract is empty has highest id 0, and its counter is
        -- already past that, so the burn is a no-op for it rather than a
        -- special case. The same check makes a SECOND restore free: the counter
        -- is already clear from the first, so nothing is generated at all.
        --
        -- NOTE WHAT IS ABSENT FROM THE ENSURES. This rule does not claim
        -- anything about highest_row_id, because it runs BEFORE the rows are
        -- written (see RestoreSnapshot) and at that moment the table still
        -- holds whatever it held. SequenceClearsEveryRow is re-established by
        -- the pair of rules together, not by this one alone.
        --
        -- THE TEST THAT MATTERS IS NOT THIS RULE. It is creating a task
        -- immediately after a restore and asserting its id exceeds every
        -- restored one. A test that asserts the burn loop ran proves the code
        -- calls itself; a test that asserts no collision proves the property.
}

-- == Refusals ==

rule RefuseUnsupportedSnapshot {
    when: RestoreSnapshot(operator, snapshot)

    requires: snapshot.format_version != config.snapshot_format_version

    ensures: RestoreRefused(operator: operator, refusal: Refusal{reason: format_unsupported, detail: refusal_detail(snapshot)})

    @guidance
        -- A newer snapshot read by an older tool is the dangerous direction:
        -- the fields it does not understand are the ones it would drop. It
        -- refuses rather than restoring what it recognises, because a restore
        -- that silently discards a column is a data-loss event wearing a
        -- success message.
}

rule RefuseMismatchedSchema {
    when: RestoreSnapshot(operator, snapshot)

    requires: snapshot.format_version = config.snapshot_format_version
    requires: snapshot.extracts.any(e => columns_differ_as_sets(e.columns, store_columns(e.table)))

    ensures: RestoreRefused(operator: operator, refusal: Refusal{reason: schema_mismatch, detail: refusal_detail(snapshot)})

    @guidance
        -- This is the refusal that will be met most often in practice, and it
        -- is the one most likely to be argued with, because the two schemas
        -- usually differ by one harmless-looking column. It still refuses.
        --
        -- The reason is the use case: the situation in which this tool is
        -- reached for is a migration the store would not perform, which means
        -- the schema is precisely what changed. Restoring old rows into a new
        -- schema without saying so would produce exactly the quiet corruption
        -- the rebuild was meant to escape.
        --
        -- Moving a snapshot across a schema change is a real need and is not
        -- refused forever — it is a transformation applied to the snapshot
        -- before it is offered here, so that what happened to the rows is
        -- written down somewhere. It is not a flag on the restore.
        --
        -- WHY COLUMNS AND NOT A VERSION NUMBER. Both sides can already produce
        -- their columns: the snapshot carries them, the store knows its own.
        -- So the answer is derived at the moment it is needed rather than
        -- remembered, and there is nothing left that can drift out of step. A
        -- hand-maintained integer has to be bumped by somebody who remembers
        -- to, and the one time they forget is the time a mismatched snapshot
        -- restores clean.
        --
        -- It also answers the question that actually matters — "can these rows
        -- go in?" — instead of a proxy for it. Two consequences follow
        -- directly. A change that touches only tables outside the shared set no
        -- longer invalidates a backup, because no shared column moved and the
        -- comparison says so. And a column added or renamed WITHIN one version
        -- IS caught, which no integer could catch, because the integer was
        -- never a statement about the columns in the first place.
        --
        -- ORDER-INSENSITIVE, AS SETS. A restore writes rows by column name, so
        -- the order the columns are listed in cannot change the outcome, and
        -- refusing over it would be refusing over a difference that does not
        -- exist. This is a different question from the append-only column ORDER
        -- the target store's own migrations require: that constraint is real,
        -- but it governs how the store's schema may evolve, not whether a given
        -- set of rows can be written, and it does not belong to this refusal.
}

rule RefuseIncompleteSnapshot {
    when: RestoreSnapshot(operator, snapshot)

    requires: snapshot.format_version = config.snapshot_format_version
    requires: not snapshot.extracts.any(e => columns_differ_as_sets(e.columns, store_columns(e.table)))
    requires: snapshot.extracts.count != config.shared_table_count

    ensures: RestoreRefused(operator: operator, refusal: Refusal{reason: incomplete, detail: refusal_detail(snapshot)})

    @guidance
        -- A snapshot missing a table restores a board that is missing a table,
        -- and the board does not look broken — the watches are simply gone. This
        -- refuses before writing rather than after, which is the only point at
        -- which refusing is cheap.
}

rule RefuseArchivedRows {
    when: RestoreSnapshot(operator, snapshot)

    requires: snapshot.format_version = config.snapshot_format_version
    requires: not snapshot.extracts.any(e => columns_differ_as_sets(e.columns, store_columns(e.table)))
    requires: snapshot.extracts.count = config.shared_table_count
    requires: snapshot.extracts.any(e => (e.table = tasks or e.table = epics) and e.rows.any(r => row_status(r) = "archived"))

    ensures: RestoreRefused(operator: operator, refusal: Refusal{reason: archived_rows_present, detail: refusal_detail(snapshot)})

    @guidance
        -- `archived` retired with task #4971 (epics.allium:
        -- ArchivedStatusMigration): a live board can no longer produce a row
        -- carrying it, because the migration that removes the status also
        -- rebuilds the CHECK constraints so nothing can write it back. The
        -- only path that still produces a snapshot is a dump of a store
        -- (`dispatch spacetime dump-server`), and a store can hold such a row
        -- only if it was written before StoreRefusesUnknownStatus — which is
        -- exactly what this rule exists to catch. The other way in is a
        -- snapshot FILE written to disk by a binary older than v106, offered
        -- to `RestoreSnapshot` long after that file was taken. (Until task
        -- #16755 `dispatch spacetime seed` and `dispatch spacetime dump`
        -- produced snapshots from a board's SQLite file, after running v106
        -- over it; both are gone.)
        --
        -- REFUSAL, NOT A SECOND MIGRATION. ArchivedStatusMigration's own
        -- guidance explains why a store-side re-implementation of its four
        -- phases was declined: the risk of the two copies drifting is worse
        -- than asking the operator to run the source through v106 first. This
        -- rule is what makes that "ask" actually happen rather than being a
        -- documentation-only intention — a snapshot this rule would refuse is
        -- refused before a single row is written, the same as every other
        -- arm in this section.
        --
        -- DETAIL NAMES THE ROWS, NOT JUST THE TABLE. "the snapshot has
        -- archived rows" tells an operator nothing they can act on; the ids
        -- of every offending task and epic do, because those are the rows
        -- that need to go back through v106 (or be resolved by hand) before
        -- the snapshot is worth restoring at all.
        --
        -- CHECKED AFTER SCHEMA AND COMPLETENESS, BEFORE THE BURN. Reading a
        -- row's `status` column presumes the schema already matches and the
        -- table is the one this store expects — asking it any earlier would
        -- report a false positive against a snapshot that RefuseSchemaMismatch
        -- or RefuseIncompleteSnapshot would have refused for an unrelated
        -- reason. Like every refusal here, it still runs before the burn and
        -- before the first row, so the store stays untouched.
        --
        -- THE CLIENT-SIDE EARLY REFUSAL, NOT THE LAST LINE. Since task #16734
        -- the store itself refuses any task or epic row whose status is not
        -- one it knows (StoreRefusesUnknownStatus, below), so a snapshot this
        -- rule missed could still not land an `archived` row. This rule stays
        -- because it refuses earlier and better: before the burn, for the
        -- whole snapshot at once, with every offending id in one Refusal an
        -- operator can act on — where the store's own check would only stop
        -- the first batch that carried one.
}

-- == The store's own check ==

rule StoreRefusesUnknownStatus {
    when: StoreRowsWriteRequested(caller, table, rows)

    requires: table in {tasks, epics}
    requires: rows.any(r => resulting_status(r) not in {"backlog", "running", "review", "done"})

    ensures: StoreWriteRefused(caller: caller, table: table, detail: unknown_status_detail(rows))

    @guidance
        -- WHAT "A WRITE" IS. Every reducer that writes a task or epic row:
        -- create_task, patch_task, create_epic, patch_epic, seed_tasks,
        -- seed_epics (which restore and import write through, despite the
        -- name) and the feed upserts — any path, present or future, that
        -- leaves a row in `tasks` or `epics`. The known statuses are exactly
        -- core/TaskStatus's four (backlog, running, review, done), which is
        -- the type both core/Task.status and core/Epic.status declare; the
        -- retired `archived` is unknown like any other string.
        --
        -- REFUSED WHOLE, AND NAMED. The reducer returns an error naming the
        -- offending status and the row's id, and writes nothing. For a
        -- seed batch that means the whole batch: one bad row refuses every
        -- row in the call, so a refused seed leaves the store as it was,
        -- the same promise NothingIsWrittenBeforeTheChecksPass makes for a
        -- restore.
        --
        -- THE RESULTING ROW IS WHAT IS CHECKED, NOT THE ROW ON DISK. Rows
        -- already in the store with an unknown status are left alone: a
        -- module publish is not blocked by them and nothing rewrites them.
        -- A patch is judged by the row it would produce. One that moves such
        -- a row to a known status succeeds — that is how the leftovers get
        -- cleaned up. One that leaves the status unknown (a title edit, say)
        -- is refused, because writing it back would be writing an unknown
        -- status. Deleting such a row is not a write of a row and is not
        -- refused.
        --
        -- WHY THE MODULE AND NOT ONLY THE CLIENT. On 2026-10-05 the store
        -- held 1,785 tasks and 165 epics with status `archived`, which is
        -- what blocked deleting epic #100. Every client path already refused
        -- them — RefuseArchivedRows on restore (and on the since-removed
        -- seed), ImportOldStore's
        -- RowsWithARemovedStatusAreDropped — but the module accepted any
        -- string, so an older binary, an old snapshot fed through a raw
        -- reducer call, or anybody calling a reducer directly could write
        -- one. The module is the only layer every write must pass through,
        -- so it is the only place the check cannot be bypassed. The client
        -- checks stay as the earlier, friendlier refusal; this is the
        -- guarantee.
}

rule RefuseImplausibleCeiling {
    when: RestoreSnapshot(operator, snapshot)

    requires: snapshot.format_version = config.snapshot_format_version
    requires: not snapshot.extracts.any(e => columns_differ_as_sets(e.columns, store_columns(e.table)))
    requires: snapshot.extracts.count = config.shared_table_count
    requires: not snapshot.extracts.any(e => (e.table = tasks or e.table = epics) and e.rows.any(r => row_status(r) = "archived"))
    requires: snapshot.extracts.any(e => snapshot.highest_id(e.table) < 0 or snapshot.highest_id(e.table) > e.row_count * config.max_ceiling_per_row)

    ensures: RestoreRefused(operator: operator, refusal: Refusal{reason: incomplete, detail: refusal_detail(snapshot)})

    @guidance
        -- THE BURN IS O(CEILING), NOT O(ROWS). BurnIdSequences advances a
        -- counter by generating and discarding one id at a time, and the
        -- ceiling it chases comes straight out of a file an operator may have
        -- hand-edited during an incident. One extra digit turns a
        -- four-thousand-iteration loop into a four-billion-iteration one
        -- inside a single open reducer transaction. This refuses it up front,
        -- alongside the other refusals, rather than letting it surface as a
        -- reducer that never returns.
        --
        -- RELATIVE, NOT ABSOLUTE. A board may legitimately have large ids, but
        -- not ids wildly out of proportion to the rows carrying them, because
        -- a counter only advances by being used. So the bound is per table:
        -- the highest id may not exceed that table's row count times
        -- config.max_ceiling_per_row. A table that generates no ids has
        -- highest_id 0 and never trips it; neither does an empty table. A
        -- NEGATIVE highest id is refused by the same rule — there is no
        -- counter position that could have produced one.
        --
        -- REASON IS `incomplete`, NOT A DEDICATED ARM. The implementation
        -- reports both the negative and the out-of-proportion case under
        -- RefusalReason's `incomplete`, with the detail naming the table, the
        -- claimed ceiling and the row count. The reason code is therefore
        -- shared with RefuseIncompleteSnapshot; the detail is what tells the
        -- two apart.
        --
        -- CHECKED LAST, STILL BEFORE THE BURN. It runs after
        -- RefuseArchivedRows (src/spacetime/restore.rs::restore checks in
        -- this order), and like every refusal here it runs before the burn and
        -- before the first row, so the store stays untouched. Predates task
        -- #4971; it was given its own rule in the spec only after
        -- RefuseArchivedRows was added beside it.
}

-- == Seeding (removed) ==

-- REMOVED with task #16755: SeedSharedStore, RefuseSeedingANonEmptyStore,
-- BackfillTaskOwner and BackfillCreatedBy, with `dispatch spacetime seed`
-- that reached them, and `dispatch spacetime dump` (a snapshot of a board's
-- own SQLite file) beside them.
--
-- What they did: seeding took a snapshot of ONE board's local database and
-- restored it into an empty store, first stamping every epic-less task's
-- owner and every task's and epic's created_by with the seeding person's
-- identity — the backfills a database with no people in it needed before the
-- store, whose rows all name a person, would accept them. It refused a store
-- that already held a task or epic, because it attributed every row to the
-- seeder.
--
-- Why they are gone rather than kept as an escape hatch: they read SQLite,
-- and no production path does any more (storage.allium:
-- StoreInUseNeverOpensSqlite). Every board has been on the store since task
-- #4916, so there is no board left whose rows exist only in its local file —
-- a leftover tasks.db is a stale copy of what the store already holds
-- (storage.allium: CodeNeverTouchesLegacyDatabase), and seeding from it would
-- resurrect old state, or be refused by a non-empty store. Moving rows between
-- stores is dump-server, restore and ImportOldStore, which all work on
-- stores.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Restated at the top level because it is the property the whole file is for,
-- and because it must hold of the store as a whole rather than of one
-- sequence at a time. A single table left unburned is enough to produce the
-- collision.
invariant NoSequenceCanCollide {
    for sequence in IdSequences:
        sequence.next_value > sequence.highest_row_id
}

-- A counter exists for exactly the tables that generate ids, and for no
-- others. A counter over a table keyed by a path or a pair has nothing to
-- count, and one MISSING over a table that does generate ids is the original
-- collision with no invariant watching for it.
invariant SequenceForEveryGeneratingTable {
    for extract in Snapshots.flat_map(s => s.extracts):
        extract.row_ids.count > 0 implies IdSequences.any(q => q.table = extract.table)
}

-- One counter per table, not several. Two rows for the same table would let
-- the burn advance one while a writer read the other, which is the same bug
-- wearing a different hat.
invariant OneSequencePerTable {
    for a in IdSequences:
        for b in IdSequences:
            a != b implies a.table != b.table
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Dump and restore are operator actions, not agent actions. There is
-- deliberately no MCP tool for either: a dispatched agent has no business
-- rebuilding the shared store, and an operation whose failure mode is "the
-- team's board is now half a board" should require a human to have typed it.
actor Operator {
    identified_by: core/Host where id != ""
}

-- == Importing the old store ==

-- Why this is not a restore. Since the managed local store
-- (startup.allium: BringUpTheManagedStoreOnceTheHostIsNamed) a bare
-- `dispatch tui` runs over its own data directory, and an operator whose board
-- lived in a store they started by hand finds an empty board. Their rows are
-- still in the OLD store, which is a perfectly good source and a perfectly
-- bad target to keep using. Three things make the existing commands the wrong
-- tool:
--
--   A restore overwrites by key: re-run after a week on the managed store and
--   it erases the week.
--   A restore does not know the retired `archived` status is to be dropped.
--   (The seed, removed with task #16755, was no better: it refused any store
--   that held a task or epic, and re-attributed every row to the seeder.)
--
-- ImportOldStore is the other thing: an ADD-ONLY copy. It is read-only on its
-- source, never overwrites or deletes a row in its target, and can be run
-- again and again.
--
-- THE SOURCE IS READ THROUGH A SERVER, ALWAYS. `--from http://127.0.0.1:3001`
-- is a running old store, read with the same dump `dump-server` performs.
-- `--from <data-dir>` is a convenience over it: the folder is COPIED to a
-- temporary place, a store is started over the copy on a free port, read, and
-- stopped, and the copy deleted. The folder named is never opened by a store
-- and never written, so "read-only on the source" holds by construction rather
-- than by care. A data folder that a running store holds is refused (copying
-- a live commit log can capture it mid-write): name that store's URL instead.
rule ImportOldStore {
    when: ImportOldStore(operator, source)

    let snapshot = dump_of(source)
    let target = managed_store_snapshot()

    requires: snapshot.format_version = config.snapshot_format_version
    requires: schema_matches(snapshot, target)
    requires: snapshot_covers_every_shared_table(snapshot)
    requires: no_id_conflict(snapshot, target)

    ensures:
        RowsWithARemovedStatusAreDropped(snapshot)
        DependentsOfDroppedRowsAreDropped(snapshot)
        OrphansLeaveTheirDroppedEpic(operator: operator, snapshot: snapshot)
        BurnIdSequences(snapshot: snapshot)
        ImportWrote(
            snapshot.rows where not target.has_key(row) and not target.has_natural_key(row)
        )
        ImportReported(
            imported: per_table_counts_written,
            kept: per_table_counts_already_present,
            dropped: per_table_counts_dropped,
            orphaned: tasks_whose_epic_was_dropped
        )

    @guidance
        -- ADD-ONLY, AND THAT IS THE RE-RUN GUARANTEE. A row whose key is
        -- already in the target is KEPT AS THE TARGET HAS IT; the source's
        -- version is discarded. So a second run writes only what the first
        -- did not (nothing, if nothing is missing), never duplicates a row,
        -- and never overwrites an edit made on the managed store since. This
        -- is the opposite choice from RestoreSnapshot's, and deliberately:
        -- recovery wants the store to equal the snapshot; an import wants the
        -- store to GAIN what it lacks.
        --
        -- "ALREADY PRESENT" HAS TWO TESTS. The table's key (its id, or its
        -- composite key — the same `key_columns` the restore matches on), and
        -- the natural key where the table has a second unique constraint: a
        -- repo is its `path`, a base branch its `(repo_path, branch)`, a
        -- watch its `(watcher_task_id, target_task_id)`. A source row whose
        -- id is free but whose natural key is taken is the same fact under a
        -- different id, and writing it would make the store refuse it
        -- mid-import. It is counted as kept.
        --
        -- IDS ARE KEPT, NEVER REMAPPED. A task's id is in its worktree name,
        -- its branch name, its plan path and every learning's source_task_id;
        -- tasks, epics and learnings still link only because nothing is
        -- renumbered. That is also what makes an id collision the one thing an
        -- import cannot paper over: if the target already holds a task, epic or
        -- learning with the SAME id but a DIFFERENT created_at, the two are
        -- different things that share a number, and "keep the target's" would
        -- silently lose the source's. That is refused as id_conflict, before
        -- anything is written, naming the ids. (Same id and same created_at is
        -- the same row — an earlier import.) The usual cause is a managed
        -- store that has already been used for new tasks: its ids 1, 2, 3
        -- overlap the old store's. The answer is the operator's — they decide
        -- which store's rows win — and there is no automatic one.
        --
        -- EVERY SHARED TABLE. The extract is the whole snapshot, so
        -- repo_paths (with each repo's verify command), repo_base_branches,
        -- hosts, settings, task_watchers, task_subagents, poll_owners,
        -- subscriptions, learnings (with their embeddings and upvotes),
        -- learning_retrievals, usage_events and retired_feed_items cross as
        -- well as tasks and epics. A table that cannot be imported is a
        -- refusal (incomplete), not a quiet omission.
        --
        -- OWNERSHIP IS NOT RE-STAMPED. The old store's rows already carry
        -- `owner` and `created_by`, and the same install's identity signs for
        -- both stores, so they are written exactly as read. This is unlike
        -- the removed seed, which existed because a SQLite board had no
        -- people in it. The one exception is OrphansLeaveTheirDroppedEpic, which has
        -- to give a task an owner.
        --
        -- THE ORDER IS THE RESTORE'S. Every refusal and the schema check come
        -- before the burn, and the burn before the first row, for the same
        -- reasons (NothingIsWrittenBeforeTheChecksPass). The burn goes past the
        -- SOURCE's highest id so that the next task created on the managed
        -- store cannot collide with an imported one.
        --
        -- THE TARGET IS THE MANAGED STORE unless a store is named
        -- (startup.allium: ImportBringsUpTheManagedStore). The source is never
        -- the target: naming the same server for both is refused up front.
}

rule DependentsOfDroppedRowsAreDropped {
    when: RowsWithARemovedStatusAreDropped(snapshot)

    ensures:
        for row in snapshot.rows where names_a_dropped_task(row):
            RowDropped(row)
        for row in snapshot.rows where names_a_dropped_epic(row):
            RowDropped(row)

    @guidance
        -- A row that exists only to describe a dropped row is dropped with it:
        -- a watch on a task that is gone, a live-subagent record for it, a
        -- subscription to an epic that is gone. They would be dangling
        -- references the board could not draw, and the store has no foreign
        -- keys to refuse them. Measured against the real old store: 14
        -- subagent rows, no watches, subscriptions or poll claims.
        --
        -- WHAT IS NOT DROPPED: history that only mentions a task. A
        -- learning's source_task_id, a usage event and a learning retrieval
        -- stay as they are — they are the record that something happened, and
        -- a learning that outlives the task it came from is the normal case.
        -- Dropped rows are counted in the report, not logged one by one.
}

rule OrphansLeaveTheirDroppedEpic {
    when: ImportOldStore(operator, source)
    let snapshot = dump_of(source)

    ensures:
        for task in snapshot.kept_tasks where epic_was_dropped(task):
            ImportedWithoutEpic(task: task, owner: operator)

    @guidance
        -- Operator decision (task #16385, 2026-10-04): a live task inside an
        -- archived epic is not lost with it. It comes across as an
        -- epic-less task owned by whoever ran the import, so it appears on
        -- that person's own board — core.allium's OwnerTracksUserBoardTask
        -- makes an epic-less task without an owner a row the store refuses.
        -- Measured against the real old store: 26 such tasks. Reported as
        -- `orphaned`, with their ids, so the operator can find them.
        --
        -- ONLY THE EPIC LINK AND THE OWNER CHANGE. Everything else about the
        -- task, its id included, is as read. The same applies to an epic whose
        -- parent epic is dropped: it becomes a top-level epic.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface SnapshotCommandLine {
    facing operator: Operator

    exposes:
        for sequence in IdSequences:
            sequence.table
            sequence.next_value
            sequence.highest_row_id

    provides:
        TakeSnapshot(operator)
        RestoreSnapshot(operator, snapshot)
        ImportOldStore(operator, source)

    @guarantee ImportNeverTouchesItsSource
        -- Read-only on the old store, by construction: a server is only
        -- read; a data folder is copied and only the copy is served. Running
        -- the import cannot change what `dump-server` of the old store prints.

    @guarantee ImportOnlyAdds
        -- No row of the target is overwritten or deleted by an import, and a
        -- second run with the same source writes nothing the first did not.

    @guarantee RefusalIsSilentAboutNothing
        -- Every refusal names which reason applied (RefusalReason, above),
        -- in terms an operator can act on. "Restore failed" is not an
        -- acceptable message for an operation somebody reaches for during an
        -- incident. Deliberately uncounted here: the reason set has already
        -- grown once (RefuseArchivedRows, RefuseImplausibleCeiling) since this
        -- guarantee was written, and a specific count goes stale exactly the
        -- way the last one did.

    @guarantee NothingIsWrittenBeforeTheChecksPass
        -- The format, schema and completeness checks all run before the first
        -- row is written. An operator who sees a refusal knows the store is
        -- untouched, which is what makes it safe to retry.
        --
        -- THE COLUMN COMPARISON IS ONE OF THOSE CHECKS. It is answered up
        -- front, for every extract, from the column names the artefact carries
        -- — not discovered while encoding rows partway through a restore. The
        -- failure this forbids is concrete: a mismatch noticed on some row of
        -- some table means the id sequences have already been burned and the
        -- earlier tables already written, so the refusal arrives at a store
        -- that is half a board, and the one promise this guarantee makes is
        -- the one thing that is no longer true.

    @guidance
        -- These are separate subcommands rather than one command with flags,
        -- because they differ in what they destroy, and a flag is easier to
        -- mistype than a word.
        --
        -- TakeSnapshot is `dispatch spacetime dump-server` — a dump of a
        -- store. There is no longer a dump of a board's own database
        -- (`dispatch spacetime dump`) or a seed from one (`dispatch spacetime
        -- seed`); both were removed with task #16755, and an invocation of
        -- either is an unknown subcommand.
        --
        -- The dump side is the half that should be boring and frequent: it
        -- reads, it refuses nothing, and running it more often than necessary
        -- costs a file. It is the input to every other operation here, so the
        -- realistic failure of this whole design is not a bad restore — it is
        -- nobody having taken a dump recently.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "Should a restore be able to DELETE rows the snapshot does not contain, making the store exactly equal to the snapshot? Recovering from a corrupting write needs it; every other use is one typo away from erasing a colleague's work. Not specified either way here."

open question "Who takes the dump, on what cadence, and where does it go? The design leaves server hosting and backup cadence open, and a backup tool with no scheduled caller is a backup nobody has."

-- Resolved 2026-09-20 (single-storage-simplification revision), landed Phase
-- 11 (task #4915): "Are task_usage and usage_events shared or local?" —
-- usage_events is shared. task_usage never reached this decision at all: it
-- was removed outright in an earlier, unrelated change (table, model and MCP
-- tool all deleted 2026-05-20), so there was nothing left to migrate for it.
--
-- Resolved 2026-09-27 (a weed pass during task #4971): `poll_owners`,
-- `settings`, `filter_presets`, `learnings`, `learning_retrievals` and
-- `usage_events` had each joined `SharedTable` in `src/spacetime/snapshot.rs`
-- over several earlier phases without this spec's own `enum SharedTable` or
-- `shared_table_count` being updated to match — left as found at the time,
-- since reconciling it was a dedicated weed pass rather than a side effect of
-- whichever table prompted the note. Task #4971 added a seventh,
-- `retired_feed_items`, which is what that pass was for; `enum SharedTable`
-- and `shared_table_count` above now list all seventeen and are back in sync
-- with `src/spacetime/snapshot.rs::SharedTable::ALL`.
